#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222300} :activity/id #uuid "29e78799-50b0-5ee7-8c3d-1f30ea789765" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049942 :topic/id #uuid "20144df7-61f2-5b88-ac4e-f707b4ea5302" :topic/math-academy-id 6379} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222484 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we'll study phase portraits of linear systems\n![](images/6f/6f6a92e2b8fb89f833268a5526876cb3af5215db0193a2aaaad8aac5790752ea.svg)\nwhen the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ has at least one *zero eigenvalue*.\n\nA zero eigenvalue is special because $e^{0\\cdot t}=1,$ so the corresponding component of a solution has *no exponential growth or decay*. Geometrically, this often creates a **line of equilibrium points** (infinitely many fixed points), instead of a single equilibrium at the origin.\n\nIndeed, equilibrium points satisfy $\\mathbf{x}'=\\mathbf{0},$ so for $\\mathbf{x}'=A\\mathbf{x}$ we solve\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThat is, equilibrium points form the *null space* of $A.$\n\n- If $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the null space is $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$ and the only equilibrium is the origin.\n\n- If $\\det(A)=0,$ then the null space contains infinitely many points (typically a line through the origin in $2\\times2$ cases).\n\nA zero eigenvalue guarantees $\\det(A)=0$, so in that case the null space is nontrivial.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c770b47f-91af-50ab-b4ff-900448268523" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14475 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1e26b239-0214-5bf7-b43f-2eee2d68bd35" :step/math-academy-id 836100 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221405 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221171 :question/id #uuid "edb1633f-44e1-5324-8048-80252c65afe5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21654"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e50fd4a2-76cd-5de5-b461-28a07c4f98bc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048853}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429959}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"} :step/id #uuid "3674acde-de47-5169-8c61-17c706504d71" :step/math-academy-id 836101 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222488 :tutorial/content "Suppose a $2 \\times 2$ system has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2\\neq 0$ with eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nThen, the eigenvector direction for $\\lambda_1=0$ gives a **line of equilibria**:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_E = c_1 \\mathbf{v}_1, \\qquad c_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nThe other eigenvalue $\\lambda_2$ determines what happens off the equilibrium line:\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ trajectories move *away* from the equilibrium line (*unstable* direction).\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ trajectories move *toward* the equilibrium line (*stable* direction).\n\nA typical solution has the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{v}_1 + c_2 e^{\\lambda_2 t}\\mathbf{v}_2,\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nSo trajectories are *parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_2,$* shifted by the equilibrium vector $c_1\\mathbf{v}_1.$\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we know the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=0$ and $\\lambda_2=3,$ with corresponding eigenvectors ![](images/47/47c0d53d1eed7289cee7d61cfaf1e8a62bccc084f445b5ca217c9b3bec78b0d8.svg) and ![](images/99/998a77eb9b491e2d1e733a0dfe1a65cc1d39759650e36c671ca1c2aa1064e29a.svg) respectively. \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system. \n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/e7/e71f95804ab65c6d60a907f33967a744b13e2c977ce4d518740a8470cc3a4e06.svg)\n\n- First, notice that ![](images/71/715228dc0a53bd16e4f6b055c9a4bc4679d8761e7a461e61f58003ffa8563e42.svg) are the equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/41/4186349824bab0f592f8e951f2b0ae7472e7879c693b0271af9e824bbc67f112.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/47/4734b2299d6f70fec85b91c2d6e74332ed722dc7522d497ef8bb4e80682d3052.svg) has the equation \n$y= -x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/4f/4f83cfd4f18ee670366bc8987a4c87b900ab9f5226fec8d18440627a0db6c6d0.svg)\nlie on that line. Since $e^{3t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n![](images/95/95c8829e56ce60e3fd877757c0063878e283f65a2da327bbd64ae80d725fbd81.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7b/7bb6a132d08721ef0d96e6e47dc3089de551e9551524f47de0af495a0b9418f5.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "16390489-4623-562e-a8a2-f603efe07e9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14481 :tutorial/title "Systems With One Zero Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "d39a58a3-4d07-53e9-9513-1e0afa489a46" :step/math-academy-id 836104 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222303}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222303 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221406 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221169 :question/id #uuid "6649e376-1865-5cb9-8892-687f80082d22" :question/math-academy-id "e-21650"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e3f0f2d-2ca7-59a7-801f-3ff937510d61" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048992}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391401}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With One Zero Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "5eb0a853-1f4a-5077-9fde-a01f3a87b01f" :step/math-academy-id 836105 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222304}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222487 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose a $2 \\times 2$ system has a repeated zero eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ (multiplicity $2$). Here, the vector field carries no exponential growth or decay in any direction.\nThere are two subcases.\n\n- If there are two independent eigenvectors (we have a zero matrix), then every direction behaves like an equilibrium direction, and solutions are constant:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(0).\n$$\n\nSo *every point is an equilibrium*, and the phase portrait is a field of zero vectors.\n\n- If there is only one eigenvector (we have a singular nonzero matrix), then we use a generalized eigenvector $\\mathbf{w}$ with $(A-0\\cdot I)\\mathbf{w}=A\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{v}$, and solutions look like\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1\\mathbf{v}+c_2\\bigl(t\\,\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w}\\bigr), \n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nNotice that here\n\n- $\\mathbf{x}_E=c_1\\mathbf{v}$ still gives a *line of equilibrium points*, while\n\n- other trajectories align with the eigenspace structure, yielding a **shear flow** where solutions grow linearly with $t$ (algebraic growth).\n\nLet's see a concrete example of the second case." :tutorial/id #uuid "f3da1055-bac8-5f6a-952c-fda37413939d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14480 :tutorial/title "Systems With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "5e2413a3-578e-5d30-80ac-4779d0c2e5e3" :step/math-academy-id 836108 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222305}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222305 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221407 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221170 :question/id #uuid "e89c64cb-e046-592c-929b-9269cb7b2758" :question/math-academy-id "e-21651"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4dd289f-95e4-5d51-8544-d20e3f5b158c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050967}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429960}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Zero Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "930d788a-1c62-5ebd-b158-c4759f94afa2" :step/math-academy-id 836109}] :activity/title "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Zero Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221471}]}
